1
00:00:00,300 --> 00:00:04,752
A pinned knight can make a capture look completely safe.

2
00:00:04,912 --> 00:00:09,365
The Queen's Gambit structure hides a tactical detail.

3
00:00:09,525 --> 00:00:13,977
White develops the bishop and pins the f six knight.

4
00:00:14,137 --> 00:00:18,590
Black supports the center and attacks the queen side.

5
00:00:18,750 --> 00:00:23,203
The queen check creates pressure on the a file.

6
00:00:23,363 --> 00:00:27,815
White reroutes the knight and Black pins the king knight.

7
00:00:27,975 --> 00:00:32,428
After castling, the center finally starts to open.

8
00:00:32,587 --> 00:00:37,040
Black takes the c pawn and appears to win material.

9
00:00:37,200 --> 00:00:41,652
White removes the pinned knight with Bxf six.

10
00:00:41,812 --> 00:00:46,265
The other knight recaptures and restores the balance.

11
00:00:46,425 --> 00:00:50,877
The lesson: a pin is only real if the recapture is legal.

12
00:00:51,037 --> 00:00:55,650
Would you spot the trap before taking?

